\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {III}{\ignorespaces The destroyed-vector audit across five variants of the first pipeline (July 30). Front-end share is the accuracy still present after every stored row is replaced by a random vector, i.e., the share the trained layers were carrying alone. High is bad: 100\% means the memory contributed nothing. This audit is our sanity check that a pipeline is not ``cheating,'' i.e., classifying in a trained front end rather than in the stored bank; we run it on every configuration reported in this paper.\relax }}{11}{table.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
